{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are generally too high versus realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (≈1.5–3.0 €/t on average) to the whole path when recent momentum is negative.",
    "Downtrend underestimation of speed (lag): the model captures direction but not the magnitude; realized declines are steeper and earlier. Increase negative slope for the first ~10–20 days when the last 10–15 days of history show sustained lower lows.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: largest overprediction occurs in the near/mid horizon (roughly days 5–20), where the model keeps prices elevated while outcomes continue falling. Use a hump-shaped correction that is strongest mid-horizon and smaller at day 1–3 and day 25–30.",
    "Insufficient mean-reversion downwards: after a drop, the model reverts upward too quickly (predicts rebounds that don’t materialize). Damp positive day-to-day changes following a selloff; cap rebound amplitude for ~1–2 weeks.",
    "Overshoot after minor upticks: short-term bounces in history lead to overly optimistic forward paths. When the last few days show a small rebound within a broader decline, treat it as noise and reduce the forecast response (e.g., scale recent positive returns by <1).",
    "Tail-end convergence: errors tend to shrink toward the end of the 30d horizon relative to mid-horizon. Keep long-horizon adjustments smaller than mid-horizon adjustments."
  ]
}
